      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}=12{x}^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2} & =12{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ [21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x \\\\ 21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+6y) & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
