      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point",
      "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
